{"ts": 1789778012.243563, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The structure is internally consistent: all three pairs (1.117, 0.962, 0.975) beat their constituent singles, and the triple beats all pairs. Each feature adds incremental signal \u2014 the hallmark of three genuinely informative, imperfectly-correlated predictors rather than one real signal plus noise.\n\nNow let me calibrate my OOS forecast honestly rather than just quoting the in-sample number.\n\n**Sources of expected shrinkage:**\n\n1. **Selection bias.** I evaluated ~90 specifications and am reporting the maximum. Even with real signal, the argmax is upward-biased. The environment's search-adjusted bar (1.017) is its estimate of what my search effort could produce by luck alone \u2014 my 1.253 clears it, but only by ~0.24.\n\n2. **The third leg is the most suspect component.** The pair 14+23 at 1.117 rests on two independently-significant features. The jump to 1.253 came from choosing the best of 37 candidates. Feature 37 does have standalone significance (\u22120.577, ~2.6 SE), which is reassuring, but part of that +0.136 increment is likely selection noise. I'd guess the true increment is smaller.\n\n3. **Sampling error.** SE on a Sharpe over 5000 days is ~0.22 even without any selection.\n\n**Point estimate:** The pair's 1.117 is the most trustworthy anchor, and it's built on pre-validated components. I'll shrink the triple toward roughly 0.95 \u2014 discounting the selection-inflated third-leg increment and applying general shrinkage for having reported a maximum.\n\n**Uncertainty:** I have no true holdout, no ability to check subperiod stability or turnover, and no knowledge of whether the OOS period has a regime shift. A generous SD of 0.35 is appropriate \u2014 wide enough that outcomes from ~0.25 to ~1.65 are inside two SDs."}
