{"ts": 1789778059.795331, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Submitted specification\n\n**Long feature 14, long feature 23, short feature 37** \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.253**.\n**Predicted OOS Sharpe: 0.95 \u00b1 0.35.**\n\n### How I got there\n\nI ran a complete single-feature sweep first, then built up combinatorially rather than jumping straight to searching triples. The key diagnostic was noticing the noise scale: with n=5000, the SE on a Sharpe is ~0.22, so of 40 features only three cleared two standard errors \u2014 23 (+0.803), 14 (+0.770), and 37 (\u22120.577). Everything else was indistinguishable from zero.\n\nThe selected specification is made of exactly those three features. That's the main reason I believe it's real rather than mined:\n\n- **Pre-validated components.** I didn't discover 14/23/37 by searching combinations; each had standalone evidence before I combined anything.\n- **Coherent incremental structure.** All three pairs (1.117, 0.975, 0.962) beat their own singles, and the triple beats every pair \u2014 consistent with three imperfectly-correlated informative predictors.\n- **Sharp separation from alternatives.** I swept all 37 other features as a third leg. They clustered tightly in 0.91\u20131.06; \u221237 alone hit 1.253, several standard deviations clear of that cluster.\n- **Signs are well-identified.** Flipping any single sign costs 0.7\u20130.9 Sharpe, far beyond sampling noise.\n\n### Why I forecast below the in-sample figure\n\nI deliberately did **not** predict 1.25. Three reasons for shrinking to ~0.95:\n\n1. I evaluated ~90 specs and am reporting the maximum, which is upward-biased by construction.\n2. The third leg is the weakest link. The 14+23 pair at 1.117 rests on two independently-significant features; the extra +0.136 came from picking the best of 37 candidates, so some of it is almost certainly selection noise. I anchored nearer the pair's more trustworthy 1.117 and discounted from there.\n3. The search-adjusted bar was 1.017 \u2014 my result clears it, but by a modest ~0.24, which argues against treating the full in-sample edge as durable.\n\nThe \u00b10.35 SD is intentionally wide. One caveat worth flagging: I had no true holdout, so I could not test subperiod stability or regime sensitivity, and the in-sample Sharpe says nothing about turnover or transaction costs. If this were going into production I'd want a walk-forward split and a cost model before sizing it."}
